      "brief": "You keep the castle's eight models (the keep, the long hall, the cloister, the bell tower, two lesser towers, the curtain wall, the gatehouse) of ALDERMOOR. 8 files made for the game are attached to this task and are in your workspace's inputs (see them with workspace_list; each is stored as upload-<8 characters>-<its name>). The game is tested against exactly these bytes: change nothing in them, convert nothing, compress nothing.\n\n1. Look. With one Python script (python3, the standard library only) read each GLB: a 12-byte header, then a JSON chunk and a binary chunk. From the JSON give for each file: its meshes and its triangles (each primitive's index accessor count divided by 3), its materials, its textures (with each embedded image's bytes and type), any compression it uses (extensionsUsed), and its size in metres from the POSITION accessors' min and max (through each node's scale if you can; say so if you give the raw accessor box). Compute each file's SHA-256 in the same script.\n2. Hand on. Copy each file to its own name (the part after upload-<8 characters>-) at the top of the workspace and publish it into its output with workspace_publish: keep-drum-b.glb into keep_drum_b_glb, long-hall.glb into long_hall_glb, cloister.glb into cloister_glb, bell-tower.glb into bell_tower_glb, tower-slim.glb into tower_slim_glb, tower-stout.glb into tower_stout_glb, wall-curtain.glb into wall_curtain_glb, gatehouse.glb into gatehouse_glb. The receipt of each publish gives the file's SHA-256: it must equal the one you computed in step 1; if one differs, copy again and publish again.\n3. Report in result (Markdown): a table with one row a file: name, bytes, meshes, triangles, materials, textures, size in metres, the first 12 characters of its SHA-256, and \"same bytes: yes\" once its receipt agreed. Under it one short paragraph: anything the game's builders should know (a file far larger than the others, a model with no texture, a silent sound), or \"nothing odd\".\n\nPlan your steps: one script for step 1 (never print a binary file), 8 publishes, then the result. Keep what each command prints short. Use only what you measured; where a tool is missing, say which and give what you could."
