      "output": "# ALDERMOOR: hand-over note\n\n**No node on this canvas played the game.** No node here can run it. Every play figure below comes from the build session's key-press tests. This canvas's own checks cover files, bytes and the page's assembly, not play.\n\n## 1. What this is, and how to start it\n\nALDERMOOR is a third-person flying game on one web page: one evening, from 19:12 to blue hour, at Aldermoor Hall, an invented school of spellcraft on a crag in a lake. The player is Wren, a student. On foot in Thistle Court she casts Spark at three practice dummies and Kindle at four braziers. She then takes a broom from the rack and flies sixteen rings out over the Merrow Water, round the beacon on the Lantern Stack and home to land in the court.\n\n**To start:** open the **Game page** node's page, click once, then use the keys. The game is silent until the first key.\n\n| Where | Keys |\n|---|---|\n| On foot | W A S D walk, Shift run, mouse look, J Spark, K Kindle, F take the broom |\n| On the broom | W fly, S slow, A and D turn, Space climb, C sink, Shift burst, F get off (low and slow over land) |\n| Always | Esc pauses (three volumes and the autopilot are in the pause menu), M mutes |\n\nIf the page stops on its boot screen with the line \"could not start: [object Event]\", a carried file did not reach it. That line is all the page will say.\n\n## 2. How it was made, and what this canvas did with it\n\nThe game was built once, in one coding session, by the model that runs this canvas. My brief gives the order of work as research first, then pictures, then pictures turned into 3D models. My three inputs show only traces of this. Two key pictures set the palette, and the painted sky crossfades from a gold-hour picture to a blue-hour one. The castle, court, land and Wren are models. There are fourteen recorded sounds (CC0), and the flight's music was generated for the game.\n\nThe brief says it was checked by hooks, stills and numbers. The record shows the hooks and the numbers: the game answers to key presses given by a test and reports its own state. Play is fixed-step, so a test gives the same numbers every time. Stills are not described in my inputs. The tests found four faults, and the session fixed or worked around each:\n\n- **Wren hung upside down from the broom:** fixed.\n- **The first cast froze the game for most of a second:** fixed.\n- **She ran off the crag:** fixed.\n- **The keep's model has no wall on its ground storey:** worked around; it stands sunk 10 m and 8 m taller.\n\nThis canvas did not build the game again. Seven nodes carried 54 files byte for byte and checked each. One node, the Game page, put the page together from a shell, a sky script and the engine. The Game page node did not hold the carried files itself. It compared names and sizes from the carriers' tables against the engine's file list.\n\n## 3. What is checked, and where\n\n| Claim | Measured value | Where it was measured |\n|---|---|---|\n| Walk / run | 1.46 m/s / 3.48 m/s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Sparks to put a dummy down | 3, a second apart (dummy from 3 to 0) | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Spark cooldown | two presses a tenth of a second apart give one cast | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Kindle cooldown | 4.3 s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Hover on mounting; lowest flight over water | 1.6 m; 1.6 m | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Cruising speed | 75 km/h, reached in 2.6 s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Burst | 119 km/h | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Held turn | 54.5 degrees a second, banked 40 degrees, level again 1.5 s after | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Climb | 10.7 m/s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Getting off | F over water at speed does not; F at a hover over the lawn does | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Autopilot, court | three dummies down and four braziers lit by 30 s; on the broom at about 40 s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Autopilot, flight | all 16 rings in order, none missed, nothing brushed; route 3.9 km | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Autopilot, end | off the broom at 215.7 s; done at 221.8 s | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Frame time at 1920x1080 (median / 95th) | court 3.2 / 4.0 ms; lake 4.0 / 5.5 ms | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Live with real keys | 65 frames a second | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Triangles a frame | 2.6 million | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Wind level | -48 dB at a standstill to -17 dB at 119 km/h | Build session's key-press tests |\n| Touch (real touch events, two fingers) | stick walks 2.9 m in 2 s, runs 5.3 m in 1.5 s; FLY reaches 72 km/h in 2.5 s | Build session's tests |\n| Files carried and checked | 54 files in 7 tables | The seven carrier nodes of this canvas |\n| Engine's file list against the carriers' tables | 54 of 54 both ways; mismatches: none | Game page node |\n| Engine paths outside the file list | 0 | Game page node |\n| The comparison itself, against planted faults | 5 of 5 reported | Game page node |\n| Size against the preview's limit | 20,341,064 of 25,165,824 bytes (80.8%); room left 4,824,760 | Game page node |\n| Engine and sky script | 888,471 and 1,593,464 bytes; hashes equal those given; both parse | Game page node |\n| Page assembly | shell kept byte for byte; two script tags; sky before engine; no address outside the page | Game page node |\n| Page opened without the carried files | 0 script errors, 0 blocked requests; stopped on the boot screen | Game page node |\n\nAll build-session figures come from one desktop browser on one computer. The last row shows only that the two scripts load in order and ask for the canvas paths. It does not show a game that started.\n\n## 4. What only a person can judge\n\n- How the flight feels in the hand: the turn, the bank, the camera closing in on a burst.\n- Every sound by ear: whether the wind sounds like flying, the spells' sounds against their look, the balance, and the music under it all.\n- The casts' timing against the keys.\n- Whether the page loads and plays in this canvas's preview at all. Only opening it will show this.\n\n## 5. What is known and left open\n\n**Free of errors but not judged**\n- The game in any browser but the one desktop browser it was tested in.\n- Touch on a real phone, and its speed there.\n- The mouse (pointer lock) and a gamepad. The code is there and was not driven.\n\n**Known and left as it is**\n- Wren's coat does not move. She gets on and off the broom without a motion, and her fingers do not close on it.\n- The keep stands sunk to hide its missing ground storey. The stone round the windows glitters a little at night.\n- The view east is plainer than the view west. The far woods are cards.\n- There is no burst meter, no marker on what a cast will go for, and no people in the court. The dummies do not fight back.\n\n**Unsettled on this canvas**\n- The plan says about three minutes of play and the autopilot takes 221.8 s. No document reconciles the two, and no hand-played evening has been timed.\n- No document names a touch control for Kindle, for sinking or for the burst.\n- The Game page node could not confirm that the seven node ids in the engine's file list are the ones this canvas uses.\n- The Game page node did not test how the preview serves the page and hands over the 54 files.\n- The Game page node took the carried files' names and sizes from the carriers' tables as written. It did not see their bytes.",
